course: everyday
lesson: 323
title: Manners: greeting every worker, handshakes, «How's it going?», introductions, goodbyes, please and thank you, excuse me, and table manners
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Good manners are for everyone: greet the school caretaker, the cleaner or the dinner lady just as kindly as the head teacher, and say «please» and «thank you» to staff in a café or shop.
explain: A good handshake is firm but not hard, for about two or three shakes: a floppy hand seems unfriendly and a crushing one hurts.
explain: Some greetings are not real questions: when a Canadian or American says «How's it going?» while walking past, a quick «Good, thanks!» is the expected answer. Goodbyes change too: «See ya», «Cheers», «Take care» or «Catch you later».
explain: At the table, wait until everyone is seated and served before eating, unless the host says «Start», and wait until food is offered or ask politely. With chopsticks, give them a try, never stand them upright in rice, and it is fine to ask for a fork.
explain: Example: a boy in Glasgow introduces his family at a school play: «This is my stepdad, Mark.» His teacher now knows exactly who Mark is.
fact: Every worker deserves the same respect, so greet a caretaker, cleaner or dinner lady as kindly as the head teacher.
fact: A good handshake is firm but not crushing and lasts about two or three shakes.
fact: «How's it going?» said in passing is a greeting, not a real question; «Good, thanks!» is enough.
fact: When you introduce someone, say who they are to you, for example «This is my stepdad, Mark».
fact: «My other half» or «my partner» means someone's husband, wife or partner, and «hubby» is a friendly word for husband.
fact: Some children have two mums, two dads, or a mum with a girlfriend or a dad with a boyfriend; greet each grown-up the same polite way, «Hi, nice to meet you».
fact: Goodbyes differ between countries and between friends and adults, like «See ya», «Cheers», «Take care» or «Catch you later».
fact: When a visitor leaves your home, walk them to the door and say goodbye, to show they were welcome.
fact: Say «please» when you ask for something in a café or shop and «thank you» when you get it.
fact: To ask a stranger, like a shop assistant, a question, start with «Excuse me».
fact: Wait until everyone is seated and served before eating, unless the host says to start.
fact: At someone else's table, wait until food is offered, or ask politely, instead of grabbing it.
fact: Never stand chopsticks upright in rice; in some Asian cultures it looks like a funeral custom.
fact: It is polite to try chopsticks, and it is fine to ask for a fork.
exercise: You pass the school caretaker in the corridor. How do you greet him? | Just as kindly as you would greet the head teacher | You only need to greet teachers | Ignore him, because he is busy working and should not be disturbed | Nod only if he greets you first
exercise: Why should you greet a cleaner or dinner lady as kindly as the head teacher? | Every worker deserves the same respect | Cleaners can give you extra pudding | The head teacher checks who you greet | It is only polite on their birthday
exercise: What is a good handshake like? | Firm but not hard, for about two or three shakes | Very soft and floppy, so it is gentle and nobody gets hurt | As hard as you can to look strong | Long, for about twenty shakes
exercise: Why are a floppy handshake and a crushing one both poor? | A limp hand seems unfriendly and a crushing one hurts | They both take too long | They are both only thought rude in Canada and nowhere else | They both make your hand tired
exercise: A neighbour in Ottawa says «How's it going?» as she walks past. What do you say? | «Good, thanks!» | A long story about your whole week | Nothing, because it was not a real question | «Why do you want to know?»
exercise: You want to introduce your stepdad to your friend's mum. What could you say? | «This is my stepdad, Mark.» | «This is Mark.» and nothing more | «He's just some man from somewhere.» | Say nothing and let them guess
exercise: Your friend's grandpa in Cork says «Cheers!» as you leave. What does he mean? | Goodbye, in a friendly way | He wants a drink | He wants you to clap | He is cross and wants you to leave quickly
exercise: Your aunt has visited and is now leaving. What is polite? | Walk her to the door and say goodbye | Keep playing your game and wave from the sofa | Let her find the door herself | Say goodbye only if she gave you a present
exercise: You order a hot chocolate at a café counter. What do you say? | «Please» when you ask and «thank you» when you get it | Nothing at all, because the staff are already paid to serve you | «Thank you» only, but never «please» | «Please» only, but never «thank you»
exercise: You need to ask a shop assistant where the pens are. How do you start? | «Excuse me, ...» | «Hey you, over there!» | Tap her hard on the back | Shout the question from across the shop
exercise: Dinner is on the table, but your dad and sister have not sat down yet. What do you do? | Wait until everyone is seated and served | Start eating so it does not get cold | Eat the best bits first | Eat standing up to save time
exercise: You are very hungry at a friend's house and see a plate of biscuits on the table. What do you do? | Wait until they are offered, or ask politely | Grab a handful before anyone sees | Take one from your friend's plate | Help yourself to as many as you like, because guests always come first
exercise: Your family has dinner at a Japanese restaurant in Vancouver. What is true about chopsticks? | Try them, never stand them in rice, and it is fine to ask for a fork | Stand them upright in the rice whenever you rest, so they stay clean | Refuse to eat if there is no fork | Use one chopstick to stab the food
exercise: Why should you not stand chopsticks upright in a bowl of rice? | It looks like a funeral custom in some Asian cultures | The chopsticks will slowly soak up the water and break | The rice will go cold | It makes the rice sticky
test: A new boy greets the head teacher warmly but walks past the lunch staff without a word. What is true? | Lunch staff deserve the same kindness as the head teacher | Only teachers need to be greeted | Lunch staff do not mind being ignored, because they are too busy to notice | Greeting staff is only for adults
test: A girl meets her mum's boss and shakes hands like a limp fish. What could she do better next time? | Grip firmly but gently for two or three shakes | Squeeze as hard as she can to show she is confident | Not shake hands at all | Shake for a whole minute
test: A visitor from Ireland in Texas is asked «How's it going?» by a passing waiter and starts telling him about her flight. What is true? | A quick «Good, thanks!» was all that was expected | The waiter wanted every detail, because Texans like long stories | She should not have answered at all | It is rude to answer in Texas
test: At a sports day, a girl tells her coach, «This is my granny, Margaret.» Why is that helpful? | The coach knows who Margaret is to her | There's a rule that a granny's full name must be said at sports day | The coach needs it for a form | Grannies must always be introduced first
test: Which is true about goodbyes? | They vary between countries and between friends and adults | «Cheers» only ever means a toast | Everyone in every country says the same goodbye | Only «Goodbye» is polite
test: A boy in a bakery says, «Give me a doughnut.» What would be more polite? | «Please may I have a doughnut?» and «Thank you» after | «A doughnut, quick, I'm in a hurry!» | Pointing and saying nothing | «I want a doughnut now.»
test: At a friend's birthday dinner, the host says «Go on, start, it's getting cold!» before everyone is served. What can you do? | Start eating, because the host said so | Refuse to eat until everyone is served anyway | Leave the table | Wait until the food is cold
test: You want to ask a stranger at the bus stop the time. Which is best? | «Excuse me, do you know the time, please?» | «What's the time?» | Pull their sleeve and point at their watch | Shout «Time!» at them
explain: A boy in Canada shoved past people at a shop's door without saying excuse me; an old man lost his balance and fell, hurting his hip.
explain: A girl in Dublin spoke rudely to a bus driver; he still helped her when she got on the wrong bus, and she felt ashamed and said sorry.
fact: Pushing past people instead of saying «excuse me» can knock over older or unsteady people.
fact: Being polite costs nothing, and rudeness makes people less willing to help you.
test: old lady blocking the aisle, just squeeze past | Say «excuse me» and wait | Push past quickly before she notices | Climb over her trolley to get by | Sigh loudly till she moves
test: waiter brought the wrong drink, shout at him? | Tell him politely | Shout so he learns | Drink it and complain online | Throw it back at him
test: shook the new kid's hand super hard to look strong | A firm but gentle handshake is enough | Harder is better | Squeeze until he winces | Do it with both hands hard
test: did u hear a boy pushed past an old lady in the queue without saying excuse me and "she fell and broke her hip". lesson? | Say excuse me instead of pushing past people | Old people should stay home | Push gently and it's fine | Queues are first come first served, so push to get where you should be
test: my mate says the new kid "crushed the head's hand so hard it bruised" when they shook hands. what's prob exaggerated | The bruise; a good handshake is firm but not crushing | That handshakes exist | That a handshake should be firm and look the person in the eye | That heads shake hands
test: kids say the dinner lady "is just a dinner lady, doesn't matter if you're rude". pass it on? | No, every worker deserves the same respect | Yes, it's true | Yes, and ignore her | Yes, only the teachers really matter in a school
test: someone introduces "my other half, Jo". what does "other half" mean and what's polite? | Their husband, wife or partner; say hello and shake hands or smile | Half a person, so look worried | A twin brother, so ask which of them is older | A pet, so pat Jo
test: at a party a man says to your mum "and where's the hubby tonight?" what does he mean? | Where is her husband | Where did she park her car | Where is her boss from work | Where did she leave her bag
test: a friend's mum has a girlfriend and the friend says "my mum's girlfriend, Kate". how do you greet Kate? | Like any grown-up, «Hi Kate, nice to meet you» | Ask her why your friend's mum has a girlfriend | Ignore Kate | Laugh and tell other kids
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the greeting «How's it going?» spoken in passing? | A greeting, to which «Good, thanks!» is sufficient | A genuine request for a full account of one's day, one's health and one's plans | An insult requiring an apology | An invitation to stop and converse at length
exam: Identify the principal purpose of stating who a person is to oneself when introducing them. | It tells others how they are connected to you | It reveals the person's age | It is required in formal letters | It replaces giving their name
exam: It is given that a person dines at another's table. Select the response consistent with courtesy. | Waiting until food is offered or asking politely | Reaching across the table to take food as soon as one is hungry | Eating before the host is seated | Taking the last piece without asking
exam: State the effect of rudeness upon others' willingness to help. | It makes people less willing to help | It makes people more eager to help | It has no effect on others whatsoever | It earns greater respect
